# The Ticket Stub in the Cedar Box

LouLou Bickford sat down at the pine desk at two-fifty-two, turned the small brass gooseneck lamp toward the window, and set the kettle for tea in the yellow kitchen down the hall.

Louise Merriam "LouLou" Bickford — Camden High '58, Colby '62 (English, honors, senior thesis on Elizabeth Bishop's *North & South* under Warren Cheney in the spring), reference librarian at the Camden Public Library on Main Street from the September of 1967 until her retirement on the last Friday of June, 2007 — has lived in the small two-and-a-half-story saltbox at 84 Camden Street in Rockport, three doors down from the small stone Episcopal chapel where the mid-coast painters' guild has met on Wednesdays since 1953, since a warm Saturday afternoon in the October of 1968, when she and her husband Wallace Franklin Bickford — Bowdoin '61, Boston College Law '64, partner at a small three-lawyer firm on Lime Street in Rockland from the June of 1971 until the December of 2014 — signed the deed at the Rockport Town Clerk's office with the ink from Wallace's father's Waterman fountain pen and drove the mile home in the green 1966 Volvo 122 that Wallace's cousin at Handy Boat had sold them for six hundred dollars in the July.

Wallace passed at home, in the small back study on the first floor with the west window that looks out on the two pear trees his father set out in 1972, on the twelfth of October, 2017, of the quiet cancer Dr. Susan Ellingwood at Pen Bay Medical Center had told them, in the March, was not going to see another summer at the cottage on Beauchamp Point. Rebecca Winslow, RN, from Kno-Wal-Lin Home Care & Hospice in Rockland, had been in the room. LouLou had been in the room. The old grey cat Beatrix, fourteen, had been on the corduroy chair by the window.

The cedar box on the pine desk — a small dovetailed thing, eleven inches by eight, three inches deep, with a small brass hasp Wallace bought at Dave's Marine on Route 1 in the November of 1971 — is the thing Wallace made for her the winter he made partner. He planed the wood in the little heated corner of the boat barn on Beauchamp Road, sanded it on the small workbench under the north window, and finished it with two coats of tung oil the second Sunday of Advent. He gave it to her on Christmas morning, wrapped in the blue and white paper the Camden Herald had printed as a supplement in the December issue, with a small handwritten card that read, in his careful loose-jointed hand, *For the concerts. — W.*

Inside the box, arranged by the date of the concert the way any reference librarian would arrange them, are the ticket stubs from every Bay Chamber Concerts performance LouLou has attended at the Rockport Opera House since the second Friday of July, 1979.

Two hundred and forty-eight ticket stubs. Forty-seven summer seasons. Six winter Chamber-in-Camden series. Fourteen holiday-concert benefits at the Farnsworth Art Museum in Rockland.

She has kept every one.

## What the Cedar Box Knows

The cedar box does not know how much LouLou has given.

It does not know that her sustaining gift, first set up on a Tuesday morning in the September of 1998 by the volunteer at the small folding table in the Opera House lobby, has been $12.50 a month on a Discover card Wallace paid off from the joint checking every February since Bay Chamber went to monthly billing in the spring of 2004.

The cedar box knows, instead, the twenty-two people LouLou has taken with her to a Bay Chamber concert since 1979.

Her sister Margaret from Presque Isle, who drove down with a plate of blueberry crumb bars in the July of 1982, cried during the first movement of the Fauré Piano Quartet, and mailed her first check to Bay Chamber the following Wednesday.

Her friend Priscilla from the library, who came to the third concert of the 1988 season and became, over the six summers that followed, one of the Rockport Opera House's most reliable Friday-night ushers.

Her nephew Andrew from Falmouth, who came to a Sunday matinee in the July of 1997 as a college freshman, married the second violinist of a visiting Boston quartet six years later, and put his firm — a small architecture practice on Congress Street in Portland — on the Bay Chamber corporate-sponsor page every year from 2010 forward.

Her granddaughter Elizabeth, who came to her first concert on a warm Tuesday evening in July, 2013, at the age of seven, wearing the small white cotton dress LouLou had bought her at Reny's, and who has come to at least three concerts every summer since — twenty-two summers, forty-eight concerts, and a Bowdoin junior in the fall studying viola under Dana Winograd.

The cedar box knows Elizabeth is the one it is for.

## What the Second Floor Does Not Know

On the second floor of the converted brick building on Central Street in Rockland, on the same Tuesday afternoon at three-oh-seven, the associate director of development at Bay Chamber Concerts — a warm and capable woman named Anna Beth Ferrini, hired in the March of 2025 from the development office at a small chamber ensemble in Providence — was opening the third-quarter renewal file on a laptop at a plain oak desk under a window that faces the fishing pier on Rockland Harbor.

The mail-merge window on Anna Beth's second monitor read, for the sixteenth line down:

*BICKFORD, LOUISE M. — 84 Camden Street, Rockport ME 04856. Sustaining $150/yr since 1998. Mail only. Spouse deceased 10/2017. No engagement noted. Suggested renewal: $150.*

Anna Beth has never met LouLou.

Anna Beth has never sat, on a warm July evening at seven-eleven, in the small maple pew at the back of the Rockport Opera House the ushers hold for the season subscribers who have never told anyone they are the reason a first-time concert-goer walks in the door.

Anna Beth has never opened the cedar box.

Nobody in the Bay Chamber development office, on the second floor of the converted brick building on Central Street, has ever been told the cedar box is there.

## What the Manila Folder Says

On the pine desk in Rockport, in the small maple tray beside the cedar box, is a manila folder LouLou brought home last Tuesday from the office of Judith Sawyer, Esq., of Sawyer & Barrows, One Winter Street, Rockport, Maine.

Judith has been LouLou's lawyer since Wallace's death.

The folder is labeled, in LouLou's neat round hand, *For Elizabeth — the summer she is old enough.*

Inside the folder are three things.

A four-page codicil to LouLou's will, executed on the twenty-first of July, 2026, on Judith's office letterhead, initialed at the bottom of each page by both women.

A small typed schedule attached as *Exhibit A*, listing every asset transferred to a charitable remainder trust for the benefit of Bay Chamber Concerts, with the small yellow saltbox at 84 Camden Street named as the corpus and Elizabeth named as its steward until she is forty.

And, in a small unsealed cream envelope on the top of the folder, a handwritten letter, three pages, on the good rag paper LouLou has bought from the small print shop on Elm Street in Camden since 1974, addressed at the top to *Anna Beth Ferrini, Associate Director of Development, Bay Chamber Concerts, Rockland, Maine.*

Anna Beth will read that letter, if things go the way LouLou has arranged them to go, in the second week of a summer LouLou will not see.

She will read, in it, the paragraph LouLou wrote on the second Tuesday in July, at three-oh-seven in the afternoon, at the small pine desk under the north window:

*I do not know when you will read this. I hope it is not soon. I have been coming to Bay Chamber since a Friday evening in July of 1979 — my first concert was the Emerson String Quartet, seat G-14, and I still remember the way Eugene Drucker's bow arm moved during the Ravel. My granddaughter Elizabeth, who is at Bowdoin studying viola, has been coming with me every summer since she was seven. The small house on Camden Street is for her, until she is forty, and then for you. Wallace built the cedar box on the pine desk for me the winter he made partner. Inside it are two hundred and forty-eight ticket stubs. Please, when you have a moment, ask Elizabeth to show them to you. She will know which ones her grandfather laughed at afterwards in the parking lot. Thank you for the Fridays.*

## The takeaway you can act on Wednesday

There is a cedar box in every donor file you have.

It does not live on your CRM.

It does not sync to your dashboard.

It does not show up under *engagement.* Nothing in your fiscal-year giving report — no annual-fund tier, no monthly-sustainer segment, no lapsed-donor reactivation cohort — will surface it, ever, in any renewal cycle you run.

The cedar box lives on a pine desk in a saltbox on Camden Street in Rockport, or a maple sideboard in a farmhouse on the West Hill Road in Craftsbury, or a kneehole desk in a yellow farmhouse on Concord Street in Peterborough, or the drawer beneath a register at the small pine counter of an auxiliary gift shop in Morrisville.

It is opened once a week, at three-oh-seven in the afternoon, by a woman who has been paying attention since 1979.

If you want to know what your best donor is quietly deciding this summer, do not open your CRM.

Go to the Opera House on the second Friday of July.

Sit down in the maple pew at the back.

Look for the seventy-something woman in the linen jacket who has been sitting in seat G-14 since Jimmy Carter was in the White House.

Sit two seats down. Introduce yourself, in the small quiet way a stranger introduces herself to a stranger in a room full of Fauré. Ask her, quietly, whether she remembers the Emerson's Ravel from the summer of 1979.

She does.

She has been keeping the stubs in a small cedar box her husband made for her the winter he made partner.

Ask her, before the second movement begins, whom she has brought this summer.

Then listen.

Say her granddaughter's name. Ask what Elizabeth is studying at Bowdoin. Tell her that seat G-14 has been in Bay Chamber's memory a long time, whether or not the file has ever quite been able to say so.

Then, on Monday morning, before you open the renewal file — before the mail-merge window on your second monitor asks you to suggest $150 for the sixteenth line down — walk to the front of the office, pull down a plain notecard from the top drawer, and write, in your own hand, the sentence Anna Beth would have written if she had ever sat in the back row of a Bay Chamber concert on the second Friday of July.

*Thank you for the Fridays.*

Sign it.

Mail it.

The cedar box has been open for forty-seven years.

The letter in the manila folder is not going anywhere.

You have this summer to read it in her handwriting, at the pine desk, while she is still at home to pour you tea. ♡
